  <title>
    <media type="image" width="40px" height="40px" its:translate="no" src="figures/orca-logo.svg" /> Echo
  </title>
  <p>
    Key echo provides feedback about keys you press; typing echo provides feedback about text
    entered in an editable field or terminal. The settings below describe the available feedback
    and how the different forms of echo interact.
  </p>
  <section id="settings-echo">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
      <gui>Echo</gui> category.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Available settings:</em>
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Key echo</gui>: Controls whether Orca
          speaks keys as they are pressed, including keys that do not insert text, such as
          navigation and function keys. Choose which key types are spoken with the key-type
          settings below. In terminals, key echo also waits for matching text to appear before
          announcing a printable key, so input that is not displayed, such as a password, is not
          echoed. Default: on.
        </p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Keys to Echo</gui>: Controls which types
          of keys Orca speaks when they are pressed.
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Alphabetic keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks keys such as A, B, and C. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Numeric keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks keys such as 1, 2, and 3. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Punctuation keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks keys such as percent, semicolon, and question mark. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Space</gui>: Controls whether Orca
              speaks Space when it is pressed. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Modifier keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks modifier keys such as Shift, Ctrl, Alt, and Meta. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Function keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks function keys from F1 through F12. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Action keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks action keys such as Backspace, Delete, Enter, Escape, Tab, Page Up,
              and Page Down. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Navigation keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks navigation keys such as Left Arrow, Right Arrow, Up Arrow, Down Arrow,
              Home, and End. Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Diacritical keys</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks diacritical keys, such as keys used to add accents to letters.
              Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Typing Echo</gui>: Controls whether Orca
          speaks text inserted into the document as you type.
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Character</gui>: Announces text
              inserted as you type in an editable field or terminal, rather than the key press
              itself. It depends on the application reporting inserted text through its
              accessibility support. When both key echo and character echo are enabled, Orca
              uses character echo for printable text typed into an editable field or terminal
              and suppresses the corresponding key echo, avoiding duplicate announcements. Other
              enabled key types can still be announced, as can printable keys pressed outside
              editable text. Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Word</gui>: Announces completed words.
              Enable it alongside character echo to have both individual characters and
              completed words spoken. Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Sentence</gui>: Announces completed
              sentences. Enable it alongside character echo to have both individual characters
              and completed sentences spoken. Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="commands-echo">
    <title>Commands</title>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
      <gui>Commands</gui> category, <gui>Commands</gui> section, <gui>Echo</gui> group.
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Cycle to the next key echo level</gui>: Unbound by
          default.
        </p>
        <p>
          Switches among key echo, word echo, sentence echo, key and word echo, word and
          sentence echo, and off.
        </p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
</page>
